{
 "id": "PV-SS11",
 "title": "Surface only, an allowance, or pipes: each against a known answer",
 "category": "integration",
 "process": "where a street's true answer is known by hand, how far off is a surface-only model, a surface model with a drainage allowance, and the pipe network",
 "module": "street/score.mjs",
 "executor": "node (CPU surface solver) and browser (GPU); raw measurements in street/raw/, scored by run.mjs",
 "equations": [
  "sagA, a sag whose drain keeps up (0.3 m pipe, free outfall): the street stands at the grate's weir head for the storm's peak inflow, Q = Cw P h^1.5 sqrt(2g) with Q = i_peak x area",
  "sagB, a sag whose drain cannot keep up (0.15 m pipe, outlet held 0.227 m below the sag): the PV-SS3 pond equation under the storm, its peak level",
  "hollow, a street hollow with no drain at all: it holds its catchment's rain up to its rim (PV-SS2), so at the end it stands at min(rim, P A_c / A_h)",
  "hill, a kerbed street on a grade with one grate at 50 m: the flow passing a point 40 m below the grate is rain x area above the point less what FHWA HEC-22 says the grate takes (Eq. 4-16, 4-19, 4-20); the kerb cell's depth there from HEC-22's gutter spread (Eq. 4-2)"
 ],
 "initialConditions": "dry",
 "boundaryConditions": "sag: closed on all sides. hollow and hill: free-outfall edge at the foot, walls elsewhere.",
 "geometry": "sag: the PV-SS3 street (100 x 4 m at 3 % into a 5 m sag). hollow: the PV-SS2 street. hill: half a street 100 x 8 m falling 2 % along its length and 2 % to a kerb, n 0.016. Grates and pipes are the \"true\" street's; a treatment replaces them.",
